1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
Security is one of those things that's cheap to build in early and really painful to add later.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:10,000
Design an agent with broad access and no boundaries, and every injection or leak you'll

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:16,000
ever face was already baked in. This lesson covers secure by design, least privilege,

4
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:22,000
and scoping access per user, agent, and tool. Let's define three ideas that run through this

5
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:29,000
whole lesson. The first is secure by design. It means you build security in from the start,

6
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:35,000
applying privacy, identity, and access controls as architectural defaults, rather than adding

7
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:42,000
them after something goes wrong. The second is least privilege. You grant an agent, a tool,

8
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:47,000
or an identity only the minimum capability it needs, and you remove everything else,

9
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:53,000
because fewer capabilities means a smaller attack surface if that agent is ever hijacked.

10
00:00:54,000 --> 00:01:00,000
The third is IAM, identity and access management. That's the systems and policies that establish

11
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,000
who each identity is and control what it can reach, scoped per user, per agent, and per tool.

12
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:12,000
Keep these three in mind, because everything that follows builds on them.

13
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:18,000
Now here's why timing matters. When you add security after the features already work,

14
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:22,000
the gaps from the original design are still sitting there. If you built an agent that

15
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:27,000
assumes broad access to your systems, the injection and leakage paths were designed

16
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:34,000
in before any control existed to stop them. A later hardening phase tends to filter symptoms.

17
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:40,000
You add a content check here, a log there, but the architecture underneath still hands out more power

18
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:46,000
than the job needs. And broad permissions you granted early almost never get walked back.

19
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:52,000
So the real move is to build privacy and access boundaries into the first version of the design,

20
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:58,000
not a patch you promise to add later. Let's put the two approaches side by side. Bolt-on

21
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:05,000
security is everything you add after the product already works. Filters, reviews, monitoring,

22
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:11,000
all layered over an architecture that already assumes broad access. The risky paths were

23
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:17,000
baked in early, so every patch is chasing a symptom instead of fixing the cause. Built-in

24
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:23,000
security is the opposite. Least privilege, isolation, and enforced boundaries are present

25
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:29,000
from day one. The agent simply never holds a capability it doesn't need. So when an attacker

26
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:35,000
gets a foothold, there's far less for them to reach. With Bolt-on, you're always asking how

27
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:41,000
to watch a dangerous power. With built-in, that power was never granted, so there's nothing

28
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:47,000
dangerous to watch. Design for the second one. Let's get practical about least privilege,

29
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:54,000
because it's easy to nod at and hard to actually do. The core idea is that the capability is absent,

30
00:02:54,000 --> 00:03:00,000
not merely watched. Start with your tools. Give each one only the scopes its job requires,

31
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:05,000
and nothing wider. If a tool reads calendar events, it has no business holding right access

32
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:12,000
to your database. Next, withhold secrets and permissions the task never touches. The model

33
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:18,000
can't leak an API key you never handed it. Then prefer narrow, purpose-built tools over open-ended

34
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:25,000
ones. A general shell that can run anything is a much bigger risk than a small tool that does one

35
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:30,000
job. The payoff is simple. If an agent gets hijacked, it inherits only what you actually

36
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:37,000
granted. Here's the mistake I see most often. Teams think they've applied least privilege when

37
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:44,000
they've really just added oversight to a power the agent should never have had. Say an agent

38
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:50,000
has broad delete access. The first weak response is to log it. You record every time the agent uses

39
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:56,000
that capability, but it's still sitting there for the next attacker, and a log after the fact

40
00:03:56,000 --> 00:04:02,000
stops nothing. The second weak response is to ask about it. You prompt the user to confirm before

41
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:08,000
the action runs. That's friction, not a boundary, and people click approve on autopilot. The right

42
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:15,000
response is to remove it. Take the capability away entirely so there's nothing left to monitor,

43
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:20,000
and nothing for an injection to abuse. If the agent never needed it, it shouldn't have it.

44
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:27,000
Let's talk about how IAM works inside an agentic system, because access control isn't just a wall

45
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:32,000
at the edge of your network. IAM decides which backends and which operations each identity is

46
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:39,000
allowed to reach, and you scope it at three levels. Per user, so a caller only ever touches

47
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:45,000
their own data and can't read someone else's records. Per agent, so if one agent is compromised,

48
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:54,000
it inherits just its own narrow role and nothing more. And per tool, so each tool reaches only the

49
00:04:54,000 --> 00:05:00,000
specific backend it was built to use, not everything the app can see. The anti-pattern

50
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:07,000
is one broad service identity that every tool and every agent shares. That single overprivileged

51
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:14,000
identity means one compromise opens everything, which is exactly what scoping is meant to prevent.

52
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:20,000
Let's separate three security properties that people love to collapse into one word.

53
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:26,000
Confidentiality is about keeping data away from unauthorized parties. It answers who can read

54
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:31,000
your information, and any leak of protected data, no matter how it happens, breaks it.

55
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:37,000
Integrity is different. It's about keeping data and actions from unauthorized modification.

56
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:43,000
It answers who can change things, not just who can see them. Someone tampering with a record

57
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:50,000
breaks integrity, even if they never read it. Privacy is different again. It governs lawful,

58
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:56,000
minimal, and consented use of personal data. It's about using data properly only for what

59
00:05:56,000 --> 00:06:01,000
the person agreed to. And this matters because a control protecting one property

60
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:08,000
doesn't automatically protect the other two. One last pair to preview. Authentication verifies who

61
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:14,000
a caller is, and authorization determines what that verified identity may do. So being authenticated

62
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:21,000
is never permission by itself. For this lesson, the point is where the pair fits your IAM design.

63
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:27,000
The identities you scoped per user, per agent, and per tool only means something if every request

64
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:33,000
is authenticated as one of them, and every operation is authorized against that identity's

65
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:39,000
narrow role. A hijacked agent presenting a valid credential should still find that its role permits

66
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:47,000
almost nothing. That is least privilege, and IAM doing their job at the moment of the request.

67
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:51,000
The access control lesson later in this domain gives the pair its full treatment,

68
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:58,000
including why you enforce authorization on every action. Secure by design gives you privacy,

69
00:06:58,000 --> 00:07:04,000
identity, and access as architectural defaults. Least privilege removes the capability instead

70
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:11,000
of watching it, and IAM scopes access per user, agent, and tool. Just remember, authentication

71
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:17,000
proves identity, but authorization still gates every action. That covers secure by design

72
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:20,000
and least privilege IAM. I'll meet you in the next one.

